why: the consumer asked a blocking question in the room and the honest
  answer is no — request 5 was name + per-language question + parser,
  and it never carried value wordings or a default. Their case is
  good and general, so the library should hold it.

  Their case: "can this be done remotely?" is a fact that decides
  MATCHING, not wording — a small closed enum (present / remote /
  either) with per-language wordings PER VALUE, and a default inferred
  from other text when the person does not say, which most will not.
  Overridable, and visible so they can correct it.

what:
  1. a closed-enum slot. The wordings per value are needed three
     times: to show the options, to read what a person typed in their
     own language, and to echo the choice back in it. Same "no words
     live here" boundary — the caller supplies them, the frame holds
     them.
  2. PROVENANCE on an answer: said / found (with the span) / assumed.
     A default fires when NOTHING was said, so its value has no
     evidence and today would look identical to one a person typed.
     Their own "visible so they can correct it" is what demands this.

not in scope, and told to them: the rule that reads "можно и
  удалённо" out of a city answer. That is domain knowledge.

